From b18891c7600ce353c9dff4522b2a67e0f16f8b7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jade Lovelace <jadel@mercury.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Feb 2026 12:02:26 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix: warn when --include/--exclude are used suspiciously
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This is a workaround to not break users, but still make the UX okay. I
suspect that inside fb, there are lots of people that do:
buck test //... --include myproject myotherproject

I chose not to error out on this case, since I suspect scripts might do
`["buck", "test", *targets, "--include", *labels]`, which is valid for
no targets and shouldn't be an edge case.

Fixes: https://github.com/facebook/buck2/issues/1243

Test plan with reproducer in //foobar:
```
$ target/debug/buck2 test --exclude meow //foobar/...
No target patterns specified, but --include/--exclude flags are set.
This is likely a mistake: put targets first, then --include/--exclude, since include/exclude consume all remaining args
hint: The following requested labels look like target patterns: '//foobar/...'
hint: Try putting them before --include/--exclude.
hint: For example: buck2 test //foo --include mylabel

Build ID: 925dd280-7479-4ddb-82d8-2f0773b40412
Command: test.
Time elapsed: 0.0s
Tests finished: Pass 0. Fail 0. Fatal 0. Skip 0. Omit 0. Infra Failure 0. Build failure 0
NO TESTS RAN

$ target/debug/buck2 test //foobar/... --exclude meow
✓ Pass: gh_facebook_buck2//foobar:test_without_label (0.0s)
---- STDOUT ----

---- STDERR ----

✓ Pass: gh_facebook_buck2//foobar:test_with_label (0.0s)
---- STDOUT ----

